So, for positive real numbers \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_k \), we have: \[
\frac{a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_k}{k} \geq \sqrt[k]{a_1 a_2 \cdots a_k}
\]

In this case, since the sum and product are both equal to \( n \), substituting these into the inequality gives: \[
\frac{n}{k} \geq \sqrt[k]{n}
\]

Simplify this: \[
\frac{n^k}{k^k} = n \implies n^{k} = k^{k} n \implies n^{k - 1} = k^{k}
\]
